</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Ablation Study on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also design an ablation study on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. By default, we set <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=2</annotation></semantics></math> to balance the contribution of potential weights in the future. To explore extreme cases, we set <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, Eq. (<a href="#S4.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ 4.2 Optimization Objective for 𝐵_𝑡 ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ PLAN: Proactive Low-Rank Allocation for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) becomes:</p>
<table id="S5.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\epsilon}_{t}(W_{t})=\rho\ \text{sign}(\textbf{g})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">g</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\epsilon}_{t}(W_{t})=\rho\ \text{sign}(\textbf{g}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which eliminates the magnitude of the gradient and retains only its direction. When <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1</annotation></semantics></math>, Eq. (<a href="#S4.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ 4.2 Optimization Objective for 𝐵_𝑡 ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ PLAN: Proactive Low-Rank Allocation for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) transforms to</p>
<table id="S5.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{\epsilon}(W_{ij})}=\begin{cases}\frac{1}{\textbf{g}_{ij}},&amp;\text{if}\ \textbf{g}_{ij}=\max(\textbf{g})\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">g</mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">g</mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">g</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{\epsilon}(W_{ij})}=\begin{cases}\frac{1}{\textbf{g}_{ij}},&amp;\text{if}\ \textbf{g}_{ij}=\max(\textbf{g})\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results, shown in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Ablation Study of PLAN Components. ‣ 5.3 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ PLAN: Proactive Low-Rank Allocation for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, demonstrate that both extreme configurations lead to unstable outcomes. From a numerical perspective, the magnitude of Eq. (<a href="#S4.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ 4.2 Optimization Objective for 𝐵_𝑡 ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ PLAN: Proactive Low-Rank Allocation for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) is much smaller than in Eq. (<a href="#S5.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ Ablation Study on 𝑝. ‣ 5.3 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ PLAN: Proactive Low-Rank Allocation for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>), resulting in <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{W}\ll\hat{\epsilon}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{W}\ll\hat{\epsilon}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> during the normal training of deep neural networks. This reasoning is similarly applicable to Eq.  (<a href="#S5.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ Ablation Study on 𝑝. ‣ 5.3 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ PLAN: Proactive Low-Rank Allocation for Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), where <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{ij}\ll 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{ij}\ll 1</annotation></semantics></math> implies that <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{W}_{ij}\ll\hat{\epsilon}(W_{ij})M_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{W}_{ij}\ll\hat{\epsilon}(W_{ij})M_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> refer to the row and column indices corresponding to the maximum absolute value of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. Since our PLAN method involves a min-max problem, an imbalance in difficulty between the minimization and maximization components can lead to training instability. When gradient magnitudes vary significantly, the optimization may fail to converge or even diverge. This issue, which is common in min-max optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, arises when one component’s perturbations are disproportionately large or small, disrupting stable training.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T5" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S5.T5.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:422.3pt;height:101.8pt;vertical-align:-48.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-16.2pt,3.9pt) scale(0.928594084441587,0.928594084441587) ;">
<table id="S5.T5.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T5.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T5.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;" rowspan="2">  <span id="S5.T5.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="S5.T5.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;" colspan="2">  <span id="S5.T5.3.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CIFAR-100</span></th>
<th id="S5.T5.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;" colspan="2">  <span id="S5.T5.3.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ImageNet-20</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S5.T5.3.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T5.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;">  <span id="S5.T5.3.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-1.1pt;">Acc <math id="S5.T5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
